  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers linger across southern New England today. \nFriday will still see some showers, though more scattered and \naccompanied by increasing breaks of sun. \n\nA more manageable amount of rain expected today compared to Wednesday \nas the upper low continues to spin overhead. The surface low shifts \neast over the Gulf of Maine today. This will back low level flow to \nthe north, eliminating the bulk of the orographic enhancement while \nthe low level jet pivots and lifts out as well. Even so, given the \ncold pool aloft (-10 to -12C at 500mb) and mid level PVA combined \nwith elevated PWATs and a saturated atmospheric column we're in for \nanother day of low clouds, accompanied by light to moderate rain \nshowers. By Friday we finally get some mid level height rises as the \ntrough lifts into the maritimes and this allows for some drier air \nfilter in reducing shower coverage and increasing the breaks of sun \non Friday. Friday's building ridge will also mean a warmer \nairmass nudging into the region bringing us upper 70s/low 80s \nfor the first time in several days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mix of clouds and sun with seasonable \ntemperatures and some humidity this weekend. Next chance for \nshowers and t-storms return with a frontal boundary moving in \nlate Sunday into Sunday evening, possibly lingering into Monday \nalong the southeast coast.\n\nWeekend on the whole looks favorable with high pressure to our south \nallowing for modest warming and an increase in humidity level too, \nwith a mix of clouds and sun. Seasonable highs in the low to mid 80s.\n\nThings turn more unsettled as we move into late Sunday into Sunday \nevening, and in some global model ensemble solutions possibly \nlingering into part of Monday. Another seasonably-strong upper level \ntrough moving across Ontario and into Quebec drags a slow-moving \nsurface cold front through the northern-central Appalachians and the \nNortheast. This frontal boundary will draw upon some deeper moisture \nwith precipitable water values increasing to 1.5-1.7\", about 1 \nstandard deviation above early August climatology. This front looks \nto offer our next chance for rain/thunderstorms, with most of the \nensemble means offering a timing around late in the day Sunday into \nSunday night. AI-based severe weather progs show very limited \nchances for severe weather, which seems reasonable as flow aloft is \nnot particularly strong and the evening time-of-day putting a limit \nto how much destabilization can occur. While nothing like the \nlingering slow-moving rainstorm, this front could still produce some \ndecent rainfall with EPS/GEFS probabilities showing moderately high \n(40-70%) probs 24-hr rains over a half inch but low (< 30%) probs of \n24-hr rains over an inch. Some spots in interior Southern New \nEngland have seen higher rain totals in the last 24-48 hrs, so these \nareas could need to be monitored for heavy rain/flooding but it's \nstill viewed as a low risk at the moment. Where there is still some \nuncertainty is if a lobe of mid-level energy on the southern end of \nthe mid-level trough axis can close off until a closed low over the \nlower Ohio Valley; if this happens, we'd still clear out \nsufficiently enough by early Monday, but there may be some lingering \nclouds/showers Monday near the southern waters and perhaps into \nsoutheast New England. That'll need to be monitored in subsequent \nmodel runs.",
